A U.S. corporate bond fund invests in bonds issued by companies. Corporate bonds generally offer higher yields than government securities because they involve additional credit risk. Under current conditions, a diversified corporate bond fund could provide approximately 6.0% per year, although returns are not guaranteed.

The fund offers diversification, regular interest income, and generally high liquidity. However, its value can fluctuate when interest rates or economic conditions change. Companies may also experience financial difficulties or default.

Unlike an individual bond held until maturity, a bond fund has no single maturity date at which investors receive a predetermined amount. The value of the investment when the investor needs the money can therefore be higher or lower than the original investment.
